{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-84906",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we will set our result equal to $-\\frac{1}{3}.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=-(3x+4)(6x-k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{k}{6}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{k}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{8}{6}+\\frac{k}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{k-8}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k-8}{12} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ k-8 & =-4 \\\\ k & =-4+8 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
